John Edward: "Language proficiencies are a crucial skill for the citizens of the world"
Scotland's independent schools have set record for academic breakthroughs, without coming down in 2018 and once again demonstrated exceptional exam results, which have only been further boosted by individual and collective achievements in the fields of sports, arts, music, and other community merits.
When more than 30,000 students are enrolled across Scotia, these schools, represented by the Scottish Independent Schools Council (SCIS), aim to provide their pupils and their parents with the highest level of service.
Independent schools strive to prepare their students for further and higher education, their chosen careers and to be citizens of the world.
While working in the education sector, which can create and implement the planned school program, we can see that modern languages continue to be popular and willing to be picked up in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, "If you are able to talk to a person in a language he understands, he will understand you with his mind.
"If you talk to him in his own language, you'll tell his heart.'
It's a strong reminder that we can't conflame only in English if we want to build relationships and build trust in people from other countries.
According to the results of recent exams of this year, we can say that the speeches appear at the peak of popularity and have the best retention ratings in independent schools.
In a total, 68 per cent of students who studied the foreign language received the highest A-assessment.
Data collected from the 74 schools, which are members of SCIS, showed that 72 per cent of students received the highest A rating during the Mandarin language exam, the highest A rating was received by 72 per cent of German-educated German, as well as 69 per cent of those who studied French and 63 per cent of those who have studied Spanish.
This shows that independent schools in Scootial consider the proficiency of foreign languages as a key skill that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Speeches, as an elective subject, are now considered to be the same as the main subjects (science, technology, engineering, and math).
According to a 2014 survey conducted by the UK Employment and Competence Commission, it was found that of all the reasons why it was difficult for employers to fill jobs, 17 per cent had insufficient language skills.
As a result, language skills are becoming more and more mandatory for young people to be prepared for their future career.
As employment opportunities in the future will largely depend on languages, these skills become essential in a globalised world.
No matter which career a person chooses to know a second language, in the future he will be significantly superior to others if he has the skills he needs for a lifetime.
Ability to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will be much more competitive.
According to the YouGov 2013 survey of more than 1,000 UK adults, 75 per cent were unsuccessful to speak a foreign language well enough to take part in the conversation, while French was the only language spoken by a double-digit percentage of people, i.e. 15 per cent.
That's why it's important to invest in language training for the children of the present.
Knowing multiple languages, especially in developing countries, children will have a better chance of meaningful employment.
In Scotland, each school differs according to the languages they are taught.
Quantificent schools focus on more classical modern languages, while in others they teach languages that are considered the most important for the United Kingdom, looking at the future of 2020, such as tangerines or Japanese.
No matter the interests of your child, there will always be a lot of languages to choose from in an independent school with teaching staff and professionals in this field.
Scotland's independent schools are ready to create a teaching environment that prepares children and will provide them with the skills they need to live in the future.
It is undeniable that in today-in-the-world business-environment, languages remain vital for the country's future, so it must also be reflected in the education system.
In fact, modern languages should certainly be regarded as “international communication skills”.
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and results for Scottish young people.
This needs to be done.
John Edward is headmaster of the Council of Independent Schools in Scotland
