"At the end of the speech, one girl came up to me and said, "Why do I talk about it only now, in the last year of my studies?"
She said she hadn't told her about the importance of her sleep, and that surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
The course is offered for the first time this year. It explains to students how the most important habits of high-quality sleep help to achieve academic and athletic results, to improve overall well-being.
Paulius Barreira, professor of psychiatrics at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services department, said the university had decided to nominate this course when it emerged that students had a severe lack of sleep within a week.
An hour-long course includes a series of interactive assignments.
There's a dormitory room photo in one section where students can crawl a coffee cup, curtains, simulators and books and thus learn about the effects of caffeine, the effect of light, how sleep deprivation affects sports performances and what is the importance of walking to a sleep routine.
