Map of the student campus with interactive pictograms encouraging participants to reflect on their day-to-day voyage.
"We understand that it won't change the behavior of the students right away.
"But we think that they have the right to know - just like you have the right to be aware of the health consequences when you're opting for smoking," Professor Czaisler explained.
Primordial culture does not slode overnight, according to him, still exists. Modern technology and ever-increasing pressure for students have led to an ever-increasing problem of sleep deprivation.
"Enhancing a sufficient and high-co-sleeping sleep should be students' secret weapon in the fight against stress, exhaustion and anxieties, he said, "while avoiding gaining weight, because sleep deprivation switches the brain into fasting mode, so students constantly feel hungry."
Raymond So, a 19-year-old califoren who studied chemical and physiological biology, helped Professor Czeisler to prepare a course when he attended one of the subjects he teaches at Harvard last year.
"He's said that the course opened his eyes to him and inspired him to request a course accessible to the entire student campus.
