Almanac: Inventor of Geiger meter
And now a page from our Sunday Morning almanac: September 30, 1882, it's already 136 years ago and MORE... That's the new physicist Johannes Wilhelm (Hans) Geiger was born that day in Germany.
Geiger invented the method of detecting and measuring radioactivity. An invention by which a device was later developed, known as the Geiger meter.
Since then, this device has become a hit of the scientific world, and then a hit by popular culture, when Roy Rogers and Dale Evans starred in the 1950 movie “Bells of Coronado”.
Husband: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger meter used to detect radioactive minerals such as uranium.
"When you put these headphones, you can actually hear how atoms work, touched by radioactive minerals."
Evans: "Hey, now it's really firing!"
Hans Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the ingenuity that received his name continues to exist.
