What is the World Citizen Festivities and has it contributed in any way to reduce poverty?
The World Citizen's World Festival will take place in New York this Saturday, an annual music event featuring an impressive number of stars with an equally impressive goal to eradicate poverty in the world.
For the seventh year now, the World Citizen Festival will meet tens of thousands of people in Central Park’s Great Meadow who will not only listen to stars such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but will spread the message more broadly about the event’s real goal of eradicating extreme poverty by 2030.
The World Citizen’s Festival was launched in 2012. It is a follow-up to the Global Anti-Poverty Project – an international medichronician group that hopes to eradicate the number of people who are actively struggling with poverty.
In order to get a free ticket to the event (unless they wanted to buy a paid VIP ticket), visitors of the concert had to perform a variety of tasks or actions, such as volunteering activities, sending an e-mail to a world guide, make a phone call, or in any other way meaningfully help to raise awareness about their goal of eradicating poverty.
However, are the citizens of the world successfully achieving this goal? There are 12 years left until the deadline.
Does the idea of rewarding people with a free-to-be-conversation will really convince them to demand and encourage action? Maybe it's just another example of the so-called cliactivism, fictitious activism - people think that they will definitely change the situation by signing an online petition or posting a post on Twitter.
According to the organizers of the World Citizen Festival, since 2011, the movement has enrolled more than 19 million actions of its supporters, which promote a wide range of goals.
They argue that, under the pressure of these actions, world leaders have announced $37 billion in commitments and policy Strategies that envisage changing the lives of more than $2.25 billion by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cited 3,000 responsibilities and pronouncements prompted by the group's actions had already been disbursed or at least $10bn.
According to the group's compilation, almost 649 million people around the world have already been directly affected by secured funds.
Some key commitments can be cited as "The Power of Nutrition," a partnership for investors and executors in the UK to "help children grow and unleash all their chances. With more than 1,700 tweets from global citizens, it has pledged to give Rwanda U.S. U.S. dollars to help the country address malnutrition.
"Building support from the British government, sponsors, governments of countries, and those like you, citizens of the world, we can ensure that social injustice such as malnutrition becomes a thing of the past," said Tracey Ullman, announcedled at a gig in London in April 2018.
This group also pointed out that after receiving 5,000 referrals calling in the United Kingdom to improve the diets of mothers and children, the government has announced funding for the Thought Proportion project, which will bring dietary interventions to 5 million women and children.
Responding to one of the FAQs on its website, which asks "Why do you think we can eradicate extremely extreme poverty?"
The citizens of the world answer: "This is a long and difficult path - sometimes we will stumble and face failures.
"Never, like the great movements for human rights and against apartheid, we'll win because we're all stronger together.'
Janet Jackson, "The Weeknd", Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of the performers taking part at this year's event in New York. The event will be hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
