"Red Flood" news. Concentrations in Painelas, Manatis and Sarasota are declining
According to the most recent report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission (FWC), the total concentration of "red tides" in the Tampa Bay area is declining.
According to FWC, a more fragmented blooming has been observed in the counties of Painelas, Manatis, Sarasota, Sharlotte and Colises, suggesting that the concentration is declining.
"Red Flood blooming" covers approximately 130 miles of coast from northern Painelas to the southern counties.
Blooming areas can be found about 10 miles from the shoe in Hillsbor County, but there are fewer of them than last week.
"Red Flood" has also been spotted in Pasko County.
It was reported last week that the concentration in Painelas district and along its shoal was average, along the Hillsbor County coast - from low to high, in Manati county - from background to high, in the district of Sarastota and along the shore - from the background-to-temporary, in the district of Charloty - from the background to medium, along the Li County coast - from background to high, and in the district of Colum.
Refrequences of respiratory irritation are still being reported in Painelas, Manati, Sarasotos, Li and Colisear districts.
There have Been no reports of respiratory irritation in north-West Florida last week.
